    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Moving into halls of residence (a dorm, in American terms) is a rite of passage. It is the first time many young adults get to curate their own space, expressing their personality through posters, soft rugs and twinkling lights. Beneath the excitement, though, lies a critical reality: student halls are high-density buildings with strict fire safety rules. Fire safety doesn’t mean settling for a sterile, hospital-like room. It simply means choosing materials and gadgets that reduce risk while still looking good. This guide explores how to create a stylish, Instagram-ready student room that passes your accommodation team’s inspection and keeps you safe.</p>
<p>Rules vary between universities and accommodation providers, so read your accommodation agreement and your halls’ fire safety guidance before you buy anything. If something is unclear, ask your accommodation team in writing and keep the reply.</p>

<h2 id="led-lights">1. LED lights</h2>
<p>Old-style incandescent string lights are a staple of cosy decor, but they generate heat. In a cramped student room, where lights often touch wall hangings or bedding, that heat is a real fire risk. The answer is LED lighting.</p>
<p>LED (light-emitting diode) lights are the go-to choice for fire-safe dorm decor. They run much cooler than incandescent bulbs, although they still shouldn’t be buried in bedding or wrapped in fabric. Buy them new from a reputable retailer and check for safety markings: in the UK, look for UKCA or CE marking (US products carry marks such as UL or ETL instead). Battery or USB-powered sets are often allowed where mains-powered decorative lights are not, so check your halls’ rules first, and never hang lights across the ceiling or over a door frame. Many modern LEDs also offer colour-changing settings via an app and built-in timers that switch the lights off when you head to lectures or fall asleep.</p>

<h2 id="flameless-candles">2. Flameless candles</h2>
<p>Almost every university hall has a strict “no naked flames” policy. That usually means candles, incense and often wax melters are banned. Losing candlelight can make a room feel cold, but flameless candles have become impressively realistic.</p>
<p>Good-quality flameless candles are made from real wax with a flickering LED “flame” that mimics the movement of a real one. They give the same amber glow and cosy atmosphere without the risk of a knocked-over candle starting a fire. A cluster of three in different heights on a tray looks deliberate and finished. Many have timers, so they switch themselves off. For fragrance, a linen spray on curtains or a scented drawer sachet is a simple flame-free option, so you can keep the look of a candlelit room without breaking your accommodation agreement.</p>

<h2 id="extension-leads">3. Extension leads and surge protection</h2>
<p>Student rooms are notorious for having too few sockets for laptops, tablets, phones and everything else. The temptation to “daisy-chain” (plugging one extension lead into another) is high, and overloaded or damaged leads are a well-known cause of electrical fires.</p>
<p>Skip cheap block adaptors and use a good-quality fused extension lead, ideally a surge-protected strip, that carries a recognised safety mark. Check its rating and don’t overload it with several high-power items. Use one lead per wall socket and never daisy-chain. Choose a cable long enough to reach your desk without being stretched taut, keep it uncoiled, and never hide leads under rugs or behind heavy furniture where heat can build up and damage goes unnoticed. Replace anything with a frayed cable, scorch marks or a warm plug.</p>

<h2 id="flame-retardant-rugs">4. Flame-retardant rugs</h2>
<p>The hard, cold floors of a standard student room cry out for a rug. However, some rugs, particularly cheap, loosely made synthetic ones, catch fire easily and burn quickly.</p>
<p>When choosing a rug, look for wool, which is naturally more flame-resistant than many synthetics, or a rug labelled as flame-retardant. US products may quote standards such as the “pill test” (DOC FF 1-70) or a “Class A” rating; in the UK, check the label and the retailer’s fire safety information, and buy from a reputable seller. A low-pile or flat-woven rug is a sensible choice: it is easier to keep clean, less of a trip hazard than a deep shag, and easy to wash. Make sure the rug never covers electrical cables, as they can overheat and get damaged without you noticing.</p>

<h2 id="wall-hangings">5. Treated tapestries and wall hangings</h2>
<p>Wall hangings and tapestries are an easy way to cover plain painted-block walls. However, large areas of fabric act as “vertical fuel” in a fire, and many halls still ban tapestries outright, whatever they are treated with. Check your halls’ rules before you buy one.</p>
<p>If fabric wall hangings are allowed, choose ones that are inherently flame-retardant or professionally treated, and labelled as such. This is better than spraying a fabric yourself with a DIY fire-retardant spray, which is hard to apply evenly, and washing can remove flame-retardant treatments. US products may refer to standards such as NFPA 701; in the UK, curtains and drapes may be tested to standards such as BS 5867. Always hang fabric flat against the wall rather than draping it from the ceiling, which can interfere with sprinklers and smoke detectors and lets flames spread across the top of the room. Keep a generous clear space around sprinklers, smoke detectors and light fittings, and follow your halls’ guidance on clearances and fixings.</p>

<h2 id="metal-storage">6. Metal storage</h2>
<p>Storage is at a premium in student rooms. Plastic boxes are cheap and light, but plastics burn readily once alight. If you want to prioritise safety and durability, metal storage is worth considering.</p>
<p>Metal mesh baskets, rolling steel trolleys and aluminium organisers don’t burn, and they give a sleek, industrial look that suits modern decor. Metal is a sensible choice for items kept near electronics, such as a charging station or a printer stand, because the storage itself won’t add fuel if something goes wrong. It is not a substitute for good habits, though: keep chargers and devices ventilated, and don’t leave them charging on beds or under pillows.</p>

<h2 id="diffusers">7. Essential oil diffusers</h2>
<p>With incense and candles off-limits, ultrasonic essential oil diffusers have become a popular student-room accessory. Unlike some plug-in air fresheners that use heat, ultrasonic diffusers use water and vibration to create a cool mist.</p>
<p>Because they don’t use a flame or a heating element, they are generally a lower fire risk than candles or wax melters, and many models have an auto shut-off that triggers when the water runs out. Even so, some halls ban diffusers too, so check before you buy. Keep the unit on a stable, water-safe surface away from electrics, and remember that essential oils can be harmful to pets, so think twice before using one at home if you have a cat or dog.</p>

<h2 id="smart-plugs">8. Smart plugs</h2>
<p>Most of us have had that moment of panic halfway to a 9am lecture: did I switch off the desk lamp? Smart plugs can help with lower-risk items such as lamps and string lights.</p>
<p>A smart plug lets you check a device’s status from your phone and set schedules or timers so it powers down automatically. Don’t rely on one for high-heat tools such as hair straighteners, curling tongs or irons, though: unplug those as soon as you have finished with them and let them cool on a heat-proof surface. Treat a smart plug as a handy back-up for small devices, not a replacement for switching things off.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Walk your building’s escape route in your first week and find your nearest fire exit and alarm call point. It takes five minutes and makes a real difference if the alarm goes off at night.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="dorm-fire-safety-checklist">Dorm fire safety checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check the sprinklers and detectors:</strong> never hang anything from a sprinkler head, pipe or smoke detector, and never cover or tamper with a detector. Keep a generous clear space around them and follow your halls’ guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Unplug high-heat tools:</strong> always unplug hairdryers, straighteners and irons straight after use.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid daisy-chaining:</strong> one extension lead per wall socket only, and don’t overload it.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it clear:</strong> make sure exits, doors and windows are free of furniture and decor so you can get out quickly in an emergency. Never prop open a fire door.</li>
<li><strong>Know your alarm:</strong> your halls maintain the system, but make sure you know what the fire alarm sounds like, where your nearest exit is and where the assembly point is.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="extension-lead-for-a-lamp">Can I use a regular extension lead if it’s only for a lamp?</h3>
<p>A single good-quality fused extension lead with a recognised safety mark is fine for a lamp in most halls, but check your accommodation rules, as some specify the type of lead you can use. A surge-protected strip is a sensible choice if you will also plug in a laptop. Avoid cheap block adaptors and never plug one lead into another.</p>
<h3 id="hanging-from-the-ceiling">Why can’t I hang things from the ceiling?</h3>
<p>Decor hung from the ceiling can block the spray from sprinklers and get in the way of smoke detectors. If a fire starts, fabric across the ceiling also lets flames spread rapidly across the top of the room, making it harder to escape.</p>
<h3 id="fire-retardant-sprays-on-bedding">Are fire-retardant sprays safe to use on my own bedding?</h3>
<p>It is better not to. Check the product’s instructions carefully if you use one at all, but for anything you sleep in, choose bedding and throws that are inherently more flame-resistant, such as wool, rather than spraying chemical treatments yourself. Washing can also remove those treatments.</p>
<h3 id="microwave-or-toaster">Can I have a microwave or a toaster in my room?</h3>
<p>This depends on your accommodation rules. Many halls ban cooking appliances in bedrooms, especially toasters and mini-ovens with exposed heating elements, and ask you to use the shared kitchen instead. Always check your accommodation handbook before buying any appliance.</p>
<h3 id="are-my-fairy-lights-cool">How do I know if my fairy lights are “cool to the touch”?</h3>
<p>Check the packaging or the bulbs: LED lights run much cooler than incandescent ones. If you are unsure, let them run for a while and carefully touch a bulb. If it feels hot, it is probably an incandescent bulb and should be replaced with an LED set from a reputable retailer.</p>

